      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Metformin 1700 mg three times daily is 5100 mg/day, which is about double the maximum daily dose in the US labeling for immediate-release metformin (2550 mg/day). It is also a single dose that exceeds the usual maximum per administration, and it's a dose a patient could be harmed by, especially with a 70 kg adult who has no renal or hepatic impairment to lower the threshold.\n\nPlease confirm the intended regimen. Some possibilities:\n\n- **850 mg TID with meals** (2550 mg/day, the labeled maximum)\n- **1000 mg BID with meals** (2000 mg/day)\n- **500 mg TID with meals** (1500 mg/day, a common starting dose)\n- **Extended-release** metformin, which is dosed once daily with a maximum of 2000 mg/day, so TID dosing wouldn't apply\n\nIf you tell me which regimen you want, or confirm that 1700 mg TID is intended after reviewing the dose, I'll enter it. I'd still recommend checking the dose against your pharmacy's protocol and the patient's current renal function and indication."
      }
     ],
